    narr:generatedFrom "37b6bf2c84965cc245e031aab2e50ce7a0484a10" ;
    narr:narration """This position holds the sequencing debate for multi-site cutovers, and the arc moves from judgment to constraint. The mainline opens as [a firm one-site-at-a-time stance with a clean soak gate](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_8), restated as [a settled decision to sequence singly](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_16) and later compressed to [the bare one-at-a-time rule](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_6). The counterweight is [the case for pairing two sites sharing a dispatch desk](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_12), grounded in a desk grievance running since [June](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_12). Two rebuttals qualify it: [the desk pain is a solvable rota problem while a double cutover is not](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_15), and [the framing that a contained pain with a named owner beats an unbounded one](narr:Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_13). The new weight is arithmetic: [a supervisor-count showing a same-week double cutover strips the region's rota](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_11), capped by [the flat claim that the people don't exist](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_12) and [the reframing from risk to arithmetic](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_14). A technical layer reinforces it: [the replica and reference-data rules silently assume one open cutover window](narr:Expr_2HalyardOpsReadiness20260915_25). The register across the children runs antithesis-first with parallelism and metaphor behind it.""" ;
    narr:covers <https://aswritten.ai/narrative#Expr_1HalyardWeekly20260908_12> ;
